From ab54d25df9d5f693fe5637bd89199974d7f29c19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pr <a@b.c>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 23:14:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix terrain seams and on_ground; harden headless protocol

Terrain
- height_at now samples the four surrounding lattice corners directly
  instead of interpolating inside a cached chunk. Previously the last cell
  of every chunk was not interpolated towards its neighbour, producing a
  ~1.0 unit cliff at every chunk border (up to ~1.7 on some seeds).
- Chunk generation and height_at share one corner sampler, so they cannot
  disagree. height_at is now &self, has no side effects on the chunk cache
  (this also stops chunk thrash for agents outside agent 0's stream area),
  and no longer panics on extreme coordinates.
- Simulation::terrain_height reuses the world instead of building a new
  Perlin + generating a whole chunk per agent per observation:
  observations() ~1.9 ms -> ~0.007 ms, step() ~2.1 ms -> ~0.13 ms (debug).

Grounding
- on_ground is recomputed every tick. It was only ever cleared by jumping,
  so walking off a ledge left it true: mid-air jumps and wrong observations.
- Added a small GROUND_SNAP so walking down slopes does not flicker
  airborne; penetrating the terrain still clamps as before.

Headless protocol
- Action is #[serde(default, deny_unknown_fields)]: {"jump":true} is valid,
  typos such as "jmp" are reported instead of ignored.
- New protocol module with a strict invariant: exactly one response line per
  request line. Invalid input returns {"error": "..."} and never advances the
  simulation. Previously the binary logged to stderr and sent nothing, which
  hung the Python client forever on readline().
- New {"cmd":"reset"[, "seed":N]} command.
- Successful responses are byte-identical to the old format.
- Python client: raises NullscapeError, adds reset(), context manager,
  .exe default on Windows, waits for the child on close().

Tests: terrain continuity, chunk/height consistency, grounding, protocol
(including an end-to-end test of the real binary), Python client.
